00:00:55.437 --> 00:00:57.610
and I guess we can kick
off by talking about

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.371
Empathy Wines, which
honestly I want to try.

00:01:00.371 --> 00:01:02.158
Can you give me a bit of
insight into into why you wanted

00:01:02.158 --> 00:01:04.330
to chat, seeing big
trends and brands moving

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.710
into the DtC space.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.600
We were watching your clip.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:08.610
You got that?

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.950
- For context you know, I
wrote one of the first checks

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.490
into Birchbox and a lot
of early kind of 2011,

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:21.360
12 DTC movement and obviously
in the last 24 months,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.000
because of the sheer scale
of the underpriced attention

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.530
of Facebook and Instagram and
the amount of capital that

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:29.954
is available and the growth
of Shopify and Amazon.

00:01:29.954 --> 00:01:34.370
All these macrotrends have
led to an enormous growth

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.450
of direct consumer brands.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.590
So I'm watching that and
obviously my great thesis

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.010
of creating VaynerMedia was
to buy nostalgic brands,

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.090
so I haven't been highly
motivated to do Gym Shark,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.750
or Fashion Nova, or you know, Hims.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.290
Like that, that's not
where my passion lies,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.410
though I've been seeing all the growth.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.640
In parallel, I had two specific
executives at Vaynermedia

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.640
that were part of a team
called the Office of the CEO

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.010
so in theory, a couple
years ago, I realized how

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:04.290
stretched I was so I
created a team to act like

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.003
a chief of staff and
there was four of them

00:02:06.003 --> 00:02:09.310
and after a year of it, it
just wasn't as efficient.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.330
You know, I moved to
on chief of staff which

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.556
has been working much
better, but I had these

00:02:13.556 --> 00:02:17.410
you know, multiple executives
that were super talented.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.220
Two of them, John Trowman and Nate Troder,

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.710
who are my partners in
Empathy Wine, have been with

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.740
me since they were interns
in my wine business before

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.020
VaynerMedia and then came to Vayner.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.290
John had left and worked
for a distributor even,

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:32.020
for a year and 1/2 and came back.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.290
So, as an entrepreneur, we're
making this macrodecision

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.700
on the Vayner side, I'm sitting

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.270
with these incredible human
assets which is really,

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:43.410
kind of the KPI of the whole
Vayner thing to begin with.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:48.410
There's this incredible push
of DTC and, as a third layer,

00:02:48.604 --> 00:02:51.540
I'm weirdly missing wine
for the first time, right?

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.680
When you do something for
20 years, and its like

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.110
the only thing you're passionate about,

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.418
you get burnt out, and so
when I left Wine Library

00:02:59.418 --> 00:03:02.090
to do Vayner, it was so refreshing

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.100
and I was excited to not
even think about wine,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.630
and then a couple years later it was fun

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.020
because I started looking
at wine like a consumer,

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.210
you know my whole life, I'd go out

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.920
to a restaurant like
they're making a fork-ton

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.840
of money off this and this wine sucks

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.480
and all that kind of stuff and so,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.200
that got fun and it
started becoming fun again.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.350
Nobody was doing anything super crazy.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:21.510
There were some wine clubs that were

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.980
doing incredibly well
but there's no purebred,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.870
direct to consumer brand
at the kind of scale,

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.010
so that's why it happened.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.440
We're off to an incredible start.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:34.020
We've sold almost $2,000,000
worth of inventory

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.270
in the first six months without spending

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.860
any media dollars, all off
organic, against my brand.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.810
We did that as a strategy to see if

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.870
we could gleam insights at scale

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:47.150
for my audience to make
us deploy the media even

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.080
in a better way, the AB testing against

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.910
Gary fans versus wine drinkers as a whole.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.950
And so, the next year
for Empathy is gonna be

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.450
really exciting because
I think we're really

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:58.730
now gonna act like a brand more so than

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:01.500
the leverage off a personal brand.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.980
- Well yeah, you mentioned that actually.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.290
I'm interested to find out
more about the media plan

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:07.080
and what that looks like for Empathy Wine

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.650
'cause you launched it on Instagram.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:12.193
Is that the most important
platform in your business?

00:04:12.193 --> 00:04:14.010
- You know, the funny part is I'm not sure

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.700
and we launched it everywhere, right?

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:16.900
You might have saw it on Instagram,

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.630
but we put out YouTube
and podcast and Twitter

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:23.420
and Linkedin and email and
you know, we're pretty excited

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.160
to go as many platforms as possible.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.090
To bring this much value here,

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:29.970
I think one thing for
everybody to think about

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.470
with direct to consumer
brands is to understand,

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:35.020
I set up earlier, the phenomenon
on the consumer's side

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:36.230
of why it's happening.

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:37.370
There's a very important thing that

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.820
I said very quickly and I'll say it again.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:44.350
There's so much capital
in the world today,

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.730
that VCs have to deploy it.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:50.290
That's how they live and one thing that

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:52.030
everybody really needs
to realize about some

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:53.810
of these incredible
brands that we all love,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:57.360
on Instagram, or pre-roll
YouTube, or wherever you see them

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.730
is most of them are actually underwater.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:02.600
They're raising so much
capital is so they can

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.860
play a game where their
customer acquisition is $31

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.060
but their margin on that is 19

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:09.740
so they're 12 bucks underwater.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.610
Our clients at VaynerMedia, for example,

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.140
or established businesses that are

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:16.170
held accountable to Wall
Street can't do that

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.760
and so, there is some
incredible things that

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.130
are going on with these
brands but if tomorrow,

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.640
the global economy starts
to start the process

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.350
in the other direction,
98% of that brands that

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.220
we think are crushing it
will be out of business

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.360
'cause they won't be able
to raise the next round,

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.720
they're too underwater,
the math doesn't work

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.610
on customer acquisition and profitability

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.810
and it's game fucking over.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.700
And so we need to have a little bit better

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:44.870
of a conversation, we need to have more

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:46.840
of a practical conversation around

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.370
DTC brands to realize
many of the ones that

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.110
we put on a pedestal,
I would say my friends,

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:55.480
it doesn't take a hero to
lose money every month.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.150
And I think we need to be thoughtful that

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:00.370
a lot of these brands are
not building actual brand,

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:04.550
they're really CAC and LTB
conversion sales machines that

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:07.750
are arbitraging against
influencers or Instagram

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:09.210
and they need to be very thoughtful about

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.070
building brand right now because when

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.794
shit hits the fan, it will be brand that

00:06:13.794 --> 00:06:16.640
will get them through the dark time.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.830
- What is a nice way for DTC
brands to build brand then?

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.460
We're seeing a lot like our
old, instructional TV ads

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:23.792
from these companies.

00:06:23.792 --> 00:06:24.625
(sighing)

00:06:24.625 --> 00:06:26.810
- I think it's a huge mistake and I think

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:28.650
you're seeing it because
they're wasting money

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:31.910
and they don't care and I
think that the biggest delta

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:36.180
is we literally, what's amazing
to watch these brands go

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.610
so heavy on traditional,
above the line creative,

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:43.711
is that the great case study
of modern creative comes

00:06:43.711 --> 00:06:46.010
in the form of Dollar Shave Club.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.890
The Dollar Shave Club
video is this generation's

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:51.370
Just Do It or Where's the Beef.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:54.980
It really is an iconic
piece of creative that

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.340
birthed the billion dollar brand.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.310
The number one mistake that
direct to consumer brands

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.850
at scale are making
right now is not creating

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.450
long form video on Facebook and YouTube

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:08.623
to build brand equity.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:11.950
And that's how I see it
and I think there are

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:14.270
a couple brands that are
actually, for that matter,

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:15.650
Purple Mattress.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:17.970
If you nerd on this stuff,

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.870
you should go look at what
Purple Mattress is doing

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:22.640
as a company versus what Casper is doing

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:23.700
as a company, right?

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.340
One is very hardcore DTC
and is growing as a leader,

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.990
Casper, the Purple is
creating a lot of creative.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:32.880
The $180,000 production
and creative execution risk

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.630
of making a funny video, a
video that makes you cry,

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:38.380
a video that makes you
think, if you hit that,

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:41.690
your CAC and LTB numbers
get really good real fast,

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.360
'cause now you're trading on brand

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:46.810
and that's the ultimate goal.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.660
- And then in terms of platforms--

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:49.493
- Yes

00:07:49.493 --> 00:07:51.370
- Does that mean for
social media platforms,

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.203
retailers, what does the shift mean?

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:57.300
- Well, it means that the
Facebooks of the world,

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.610
the Googles of the world
are getting more of their,

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:04.120
I mean it's crazy, ou have
two gals in the middle

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.380
of nowhere, starting a direct to consumer

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:09.550
peanut butter brand and two
years later, spending more money

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.946
on Facebook and Instagram than does.

00:08:11.946 --> 00:08:12.779
- Mhm

00:08:12.779 --> 00:08:15.380
- Like if you go talk to
Facebook executives that

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:18.810
run their business, they're
getting way bigger budgets

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.410
from companies you've never heard of

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:21.870
and that is probably my favorite thing

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:22.920
that's kind of going on.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:26.460
Literally, literally, when
you look at the top spenders

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:29.550
on these platforms, it
will blow your mind.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.670
Literally a brand you've never heard of

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:35.630
and that's because one
trades on sales data

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:39.628
and another one trades on
internal MMMs or reporting.

00:08:39.628 --> 00:08:41.530
- Coming back to your earlier
point, is there danger

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:43.360
in building your whole
brand on one platform?

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:44.333
- Yes.

00:08:44.333 --> 00:08:46.160
- What's the danger of that?

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.420
- You become one dimensional
and a one dimensional

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:50.030
fighter is vulnerable.

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:51.855
(chuckling)

00:08:51.855 --> 00:08:52.688
- Well what I resear--

00:08:52.688 --> 00:08:55.410
- And again, instead of
just making a fun statement,

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:56.770
let me add a little bit to that.

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.220
Look, every time you're
looking for growth, you know,

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.220
this is actually a fun
way to create some clarity

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:02.750
about how I think about the world.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:05.400
I don't hate traditional media.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.320
I think over a long period of
time, it became overpriced.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.640
I actually love outdoor, for example.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.137
Outdoor gets seen, you know?

00:09:12.137 --> 00:09:13.510
Not everybody's looking at their

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:16.260
phone every second and if you really like,

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.940
I love UIing apps in real life

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:21.040
so I like outdoor quite a bit.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.860
I just like to buy it when it's remnant

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:25.440
and I can buy it for one
month for 2000 bucks instead

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.220
of the 24 thousand they want when

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:28.280
you buy it for six month contract.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:32.567
So to me, the danger of
being one dimensional is,

00:09:32.567 --> 00:09:35.437
why would you wanna be
one dimensional, right?

00:09:35.437 --> 00:09:39.320
You're gonna squeeze the
crap out of one fruit

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:40.790
and you've squeezed all
the orange juice out

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:42.530
of the orange but there's
a grapefruit right here

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:45.200
and you should start, and even
if the grapefruit's small,

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.810
there's still juice in there and so to me,

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:50.880
I think about overpriced
and underpriced attention

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.500
and as real estate prices
come down in a soft economy

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.650
and more internet business,
inevitably its gonna turn

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:01.360
and storefronts are gonna be a good buy.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:02.760
So, there's a bunch of companies
that are about to go out

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.620
of business because they signed
big leases seven years ago,

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:08.550
didn't factor in e-com,
and they're underwater,

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.720
they'll go, demand for retail,

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:12.830
everyone's a headline reader like

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:14.130
don't buy retail, it's the internet.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.160
So, what will happen is
demand will go down so

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.670
retail, in two to three
years, especially if

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:20.060
the economy gets soft, is gonna be

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:21.540
a fucking deal for these brands that

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:22.820
get walk in traffic.

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:24.700
Still, I mean everybody
here bought something

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:29.180
in the store recently, so
yeah, there's just no reason

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:30.084
to be one dimensional.

00:10:30.084 --> 00:10:32.050
- Mhm, What do you think
about these brands moving

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:33.460
into physical retail spaces?

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:35.130
Hardee's in the U.K.
has just done a big deal

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:35.963
with Boots pharmacy.

00:10:35.963 --> 00:10:36.920
- I saw.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:37.753
I'm into it.

00:10:37.753 --> 00:10:39.190
I think you expand your business,

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:41.477
you've got the leverage as a DTC brand.

00:10:41.477 --> 00:10:44.800
You know, the old world
was, you started a brand

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.460
and you fucking paid to get
even a prayer of getting in.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:51.600
Now, you build a $100 million
business direct to consumer

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:53.160
and you're going in with leverage.

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:55.100
This is one big game of leverage.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:56.677
- Well, I want to take some
questions from our audience

00:10:56.677 --> 00:11:00.968
if there are any, before
Gary has to catch his flight

00:11:00.968 --> 00:11:02.138
We'll just do it now.

00:11:02.138 --> 00:11:03.600
- [Man] I'll ask a question.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:06.961
Your recent campaign with K
Swiss, you were out, I saw them.

